{
  "_comment": "Source of truth for the synthetic credit rating tables. The pipeline selects a table by market capitalisation, writes all 45 cells into RefData!A4:C18, sets RefData!B22:B24, then recalculates. Valuation!D120 verifies the selection, D115 verifies the sort order, D122 verifies the vintage.",
  "source": "Damodaran, 'Ratings, Interest Coverage Ratios and Default Spreads'",
  "vintage": "2026-01",
  "next_expected": "2027-01",
  "size_cutoff": {
    "eur_millions": 4300,
    "basis": "Damodaran's USD 5bn threshold converted at EURUSD 1.16 (2026-08-28) and FIXED in EUR. Review each February alongside the tables. Do not float this with the exchange rate: it would reclassify companies, and change their valuations, for no economic reason.",
    "applies_to": "Inputs!B16, market capitalisation in EUR millions"
  },
  "write_target": {
    "coverage_threshold": "RefData!A4:A18",
    "rating_label": "RefData!B4:B18",
    "default_spread": "RefData!C4:C18",
    "active_table": "RefData!B22",
    "vintage": "RefData!B23",
    "cutoff": "RefData!B24"
  },
  "invariants": [
    "Exactly 15 rows per table.",
    "coverage_from must be written ascending; Valuation!D39 and D40 use MATCH(...,1), which returns a silently wrong spread on unsorted data rather than an error.",
    "Row 4 keeps the -100000 sentinel so that any coverage ratio, however negative, resolves to a rating.",
    "Spreads are fractions. RefData carries the percentage formatting already, so write values without formatting.",
    "The two ladders cross over near 1.2x coverage: above it SMALL charges more than LARGE, below it SMALL charges less. Selecting the wrong table is not conservative in a predictable direction."
  ],
